course: dictionary
lesson: 154
title: Afraid: fear and polite news
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Afraid means feeling fear. You feel afraid when you think something bad might happen. «A girl in Vancouver was so afraid of the dentist that she hid her toothache for months.» Afraid of something means scared of it, so she was scared of the dentist.
explain: Afraid is an adjective, and it comes after a verb: be afraid, feel afraid, or get afraid. Say «The puppy was afraid», not «the afraid puppy» in the same way. Check the sentence: does afraid fit after the verb?
explain: Afraid can also be polite. «I'm afraid I can't come» means sorry, I can't come. It softens bad news, so the other person does not feel hurt as much. Being afraid is normal and helps keep you safe, and you can always talk to a trusted adult about your fears.
fact: Afraid means feeling fear, scared that something bad might happen.
fact: «Afraid of» means scared of something, as in afraid of the dark.
fact: «I'm afraid» is a polite way to give sorry or disappointing news: «I'm afraid we are closed.»
fact: «Being frightened does not make someone a coward; courage means doing the right thing while feeling afraid.»
exercise: «A girl in Vancouver was so afraid of the dentist that she hid her toothache for months.» What does afraid mean here? | Very tired after a long day | Happy to see the dentist | *Scared of something bad happening | Angry with her friends
exercise: «Being frightened does not make someone a coward; courage means doing the right thing while feeling afraid.» What does courage mean in this sentence? | *Doing the right thing even when you feel afraid | Running away from every problem | Shouting loudly at everyone | Never feeling any fear at all
exercise: «I'm afraid we are closed.» What is the speaker doing? | *Giving sorry news politely | Shouting at a customer | Telling a silly joke | Asking for help with a hard sum
test: Complete the sentence: The puppy was ___ of the loud fireworks. | glum | ancient | *afraid | famished
test: «I'm afraid I can't come to your party.» What is the best meaning? | *I am sorry, and I am not able to come | I am coming for sure | I am hungry for cake | I am scared of the party
exam: Which sentence uses afraid correctly? | *The child was afraid, so she ran home. | The child afraid was home. | The afraid child ran home. | Afraid child home ran.
